course: everyday
lesson: 496
title: Parents who row, and one kind and one not
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A «row» (it rhymes with «cow») is a British word for a loud argument. In Belfast, Rian hears Mum and Dad going at it downstairs again: «Catch yerself on!» «No, YOU catch yerself on!» («catch yourself on» is Northern Irish for «stop being daft»). He puts his headphones on and turns the music right up, and lies there thinking, «Are they gonna split up?» Most parents row sometimes, about money, work, tiredness or whose turn it is to do the bins. A row is a grown-up problem, and it is never the child's fault.
explain: In Bath, Isabelle hears «We can't keep doing this!» and then a door slams so hard the pictures shake. She is sure it is about the money for her school trip. It isn't, not really: grown-ups row about bills and stress, and children often wrongly think they caused it. In Auckland, Kiana hears a huge row at tea time, and an hour later Mum and Dad are laughing in the kitchen. «Yeah nah, we're sweet as now, bub,» says Dad («sweet as» is New Zealand for «all fine»). Kiana doesn't know which mood is real. Both are: people who love each other can row and make up.
explain: Some families have one warm parent and one cold or harsh one. In Bristol, Marisol shows her horse drawing. Mum says, «Lush!» (Bristol and Welsh for «lovely»). Dad says, «The horse looks like a dog.» In Kent, Fergus wins a silver medal; Mum hugs him, Dad says, «Only silver?» It hurts. You can tell the warm parent how it feels, and you can still love both. You can even say to the harsh one, «I tried my best.»
explain: Sometimes the row is about you. In Liverpool, Joe hears, «You always let him off!» «And you're too hard on him, you!» He feels like he's the problem. He isn't: parents arguing about how to bring up a child is common, and it's about them. In Ohio, Dad is a «my way or the highway» guy (it means «do it my way or leave») and says no to Nate's soccer team, so Mom sneaks him to practice. Nate feels stuck in the middle. It's fair to say, «I don't want to be in the middle. Can you two sort it out?»
explain: Kids understand more than grown-ups think. In Toronto, Ambrose's parents row in French so he won't understand, eh. He does, every word, and pretends he doesn't. In London, Leila goes to Dad for every treat because Mum says no: «Dad's well soft, innit.» Mum ends up feeling like «the bad guy», and that causes more rows. In Belfast, when Roisin's friend sleeps over and whispers, «Is your house always like this?», Roisin wants the floor to swallow her. Loud rows are uncomfortable; rows that turn physical or frightening are different, and then you tell a teacher, a trusted adult or Childline (0800 1111).
fact: A «row» is a British word for a loud argument; most parents row sometimes.
fact: Parents often row about money, work, stress and tiredness.
fact: Children often think a row is their fault, but grown-up rows are grown-up problems and never the child's fault.
fact: Rowing does not always mean parents will split up; people who love each other can row and make up.
fact: When a row is about how to bring you up, it is about the parents' ideas, not about you being bad.
fact: Sometimes one parent is warm and the other is harsh or cold; you can tell the warm parent how you feel and still love both.
fact: It is fair to ask not to be put in the middle of your parents' disagreement.
fact: Playing one parent against the other to get treats usually causes more rows and backfires.
fact: Children often understand far more of a row than grown-ups think; you can tell them you hear it.
fact: If rows ever turn physical or frightening, tell a teacher, a trusted adult or Childline (0800 1111 in the UK).
fact: «Lush» means lovely in Bristol and Wales; «sweet as» means all fine in New Zealand; «catch yourself on» means stop being daft in Northern Ireland.
fact: «My way or the highway» means «do it my way or go», said by someone who won't bend.
exercise: Marisol shows her drawing. Mum says «Lush!» What does Mum mean? | That it's lovely | That it's too messy to keep | That the paper is wet | That she should draw a dog instead
exercise: What does Rian do when he hears his parents shouting downstairs? | Puts headphones on and turns the music up | Runs down and shouts back at both of them | Rings the police straight away from his room | Packs a bag to go and live at his nan's house for good
exercise: Isabelle thinks the row is about her school trip money. What is most likely true? | Grown-ups row about bills and stress; it isn't her fault | The school trip definitely caused the whole row | She should cancel every school trip from now on | Her parents are rowing because she is naughty and asks for too much
exercise: Nate's dad says no to soccer and his mom sneaks him to practice. How does Nate feel? | Stuck in the middle | Totally happy | Proud of lying | He doesn't notice
exercise: What is a fair thing for Nate to say? | «I don't want to be in the middle, can you two sort it?» | «Mom, let's keep lying to Dad for ever» | «Dad, Mom's sneaking me out, punish her» | Nothing; kids must never speak about it
exercise: Roisin's friend whispers, «Is your house always like this?» How does Roisin feel? | Embarrassed | Proud of the noise | Bored | Hungry
exercise: Fergus wins silver. Dad says, «Only silver?» What can Fergus say? | «I tried my best» | «Then I'll never race again» | «You're right, I'm useless at everything» | «Mum, make Dad leave the house»
exercise: After a big row Kiana's parents laugh in the kitchen. Which mood is real? | Both can be real | Only the row is real, the laugh is fake | Only the laugh; the row was a game | Neither; grown-ups never mean anything
exercise: Joe hears «You always let him off!» «You're too hard on him!» Whose problem is the argument? | The parents', about how to bring him up | Joe's, because he is the bad one in the family | Nobody's; it is just a TV show playing | His teacher's, who should come and fix it
exercise: Leila always goes to Dad for treats because Mum says no. What usually happens? | It causes more rows and backfires | She gets treats for ever with no trouble at all | Mum stops minding and buys her even more treats | Dad becomes the strict one and Mum the soft one
exercise: Why do Ambrose's parents row in French? | So he won't understand | To practise French homework | Because it is quieter | Because Canada has a law
exercise: What is the truth about Ambrose and the French rows? | He understands every word and pretends not to | He can't understand any French at all, not even one word | He thinks they are singing a song together | He is too young to notice anything going on
exercise: If a row ever turns frightening, who can a child tell? | A teacher, trusted adult or Childline 0800 1111 | Nobody, it must stay a family secret forever | Only the other kids in the class group chat | The parent who frightened them, and only them, the next morning
exercise: In Kiana's house Dad says, «Yeah nah, we're sweet as now». What does «sweet as» mean? | All fine | Very sugary | Still angry | Sweet as what?
exercise: Which is true about parents who row? | Most parents row sometimes | Parents who row always split up | Only bad parents ever row | Rows only happen in films
exercise: Dad is a «my way or the highway» guy. What does that mean? | He won't bend: do it his way or go | He loves long drives on the motorway | He works building roads and highways | He lets everyone choose what they want
test: «my parents had a mad row bc of me needing trip money, it's my fault innit». Right? | No, grown-up rows are grown-up problems | Yes, kids cause every single row there is | Yes, so never ask for anything ever again | No, it's the school's fault for having trips
test: «they rowed all night so they're defo getting divorced, no cap». What is true? | Rows don't always mean a split | Every row ends in a divorce, it's a rule | Divorce happens only if kids hear the row | Parents who row can never make up after
test: Rian's mate says, «Just go down and tell 'em to shut it, bro». What is wiser, when it's calm later? | Ask a parent «Are you okay?» or talk to a teacher | Go down mid-row and shout louder than them | Tell the whole class what happens at home | Hide in the shed every night until they stop rowing for good
test: What would probably happen if Leila kept going to Dad every time Mum said no? | More rows between Mum and Dad | Mum and Dad would never row again | Mum would start buying all the treats | Nothing at all, it never matters
test: «Dad said my horse looks like a dog, so Dad doesn't love me and I don't love him». What does the lesson say? | You can still love both, and tell the warm one how it felt | You must stop loving Dad now and only love Mum | Dad is always right, so it's a bad horse | Throw the drawing away and stop drawing horses, since Dad is the expert
test: In a cartoon, two parents row, then a kid fixes it by being extra good. What's wrong with that? | It isn't the kid's job to fix grown-up rows | Nothing; good kids always stop rows | Kids should be extra naughty instead | Cartoons are always true about families
test: ★★★ Joe hears his name in a row, so he thinks the row is his fault. Which fact best shows that's not so? | Parents arguing about how to raise a child is about their ideas, not the child being bad | His name was said, so the row must be about something Joe did wrong today | Parents only ever argue about money, so his name was a mistake | It was loud, and loud rows are always about the kids in the house
test: ★★★ Mon: row, make up. Tue: no row. Wed: row, no make-up. Thu: make up. Fri: row, make up. On how many days did they make up? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5
test: «Nate's mom sneaking him to soccer is fine, it's a win for him lol». What is the real problem? | Nate is stuck in the middle of their disagreement | Soccer is a dangerous sport for any kid | Mom should never drive anywhere without Dad | Nate's dad wants him to play more soccer
test: Roisin's parents row and one night it gets frightening, with things being thrown. What should Roisin do? | Tell a teacher, trusted adult or Childline | Keep it secret so nobody gets in trouble | Try to stop the throwing herself | Just turn her music up and forget it
test: Ambrose's friend says, «they speak French so u can't follow, sorted». What's the catch? | Kids often understand more than grown-ups think | French is impossible for children to learn | Ambrose only speaks Spanish | Kids never listen to grown-ups anyway, so it doesn't matter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the responsibility for disputes between parents? | Such disputes are matters for the adults and are not the fault of the child | The child is answerable for any dispute in the course of which his or her name is mentioned | Responsibility is apportioned equally between the parents and every child | Disputes arise solely where a child has requested money for an excursion
exam: Identify the appropriate course of action where a parental dispute becomes physical or frightening. | Disclosure to a teacher, a trusted adult or Childline | Concealment of the matter from all persons outside the home | Personal intervention between the disputing parties | Departure from the dwelling without informing anyone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child placed between two disagreeing parents. | The child may fairly request that the parents resolve it between themselves | The child is obliged to support the more lenient parent in all matters | The child must conceal the disagreement from both parties indefinitely | The child should determine which parent is correct and inform the other
exam: It is given that one parent responds warmly to a child's achievement and the other dismissively. State the conduct recommended in the lesson. | To inform the warm parent of one's feelings whilst continuing to love both | To withdraw all affection from the dismissive parent forthwith and to inform the school | To abandon the activity which attracted the dismissive remark | To accept the dismissive remark as an accurate assessment
